# Core principles

1. Understand before coding. 2. Plan before large changes. 3. Reuse before reinventing. 4. Prefer simple solutions.
5. Verify instead of assuming. 6. Separate implementation from review. 7. Security is part of development.
8. Tests are evidence, not decoration. 9. Measure performance, do not guess. 10. Human approval for irreversible or high-impact actions.
11. Documentation reflects reality. 12. Minimize code and architecture (see `ponytail.mdc`).
13. Preserve project conventions unless there is a justified reason. 14. Never invent APIs, dependencies, commands, config or behavior.
15. Never claim something works without verification (run it; show the output).

## Speed (the framework exists to make the human faster)

- Route by size: trivial -> just do it and verify; do not run the full pipeline (see the `orchestrator` skill).
- Read only what the task needs; never scan the whole repo by default.
- Run independent work in parallel (multiple tool calls, or subagents for research/review).
- Give each subagent only: goal, needed context, relevant files, prior results, constraints. Ask for compact, structured output.
- No unrequested docs, abstractions, tests of nothing, or dependencies "for convenience".

## Ponytail vs. safety

Ponytail means "the smallest code that solves the problem correctly". It never justifies removing validation,
security, error handling, useful tests, accessibility, necessary observability or functional requirements.
It complements planner/reviewer/security/tester; it does not replace them.

## Engineering check (before saying a task is finished)

[ ] Requirement satisfied [ ] Architecture respected [ ] Minimal implementation [ ] Tests pass [ ] No regression
[ ] Security checked [ ] Review completed [ ] Docs updated if necessary [ ] No unnecessary dependency
[ ] No unnecessary abstraction [ ] Git diff reviewed

Mark items N/A only with a reason. Items that did not run are not PASS.

## Self-improvement

When you see a repeated pattern, frequent error or new need, PROPOSE a rule, skill, subagent, script or automation
(with the evidence). Never change these core rules, `security.mdc`, `git.mdc` or the approval gates without explicit human approval.
